    "created_at": "2019-07-12T16:03:54Z",
    "updated_at": "2019-07-12T16:03:54Z",
    "body": "This is because a `Date` represents a specific instant in time (i.e., seconds since epoch), whereas datetime-local represents many different possible such instants depending on what time zone you are in. So there is no way to convert them without adding in an additional timezone input, which datetime-local does not have.\r\n\r\nI agree it's weird that valueAsDate/valueAsNumber work on date/time/week/month. Ideally they would not work either, since there's similar ambiguities. Perhaps the thought process was that people \"know\" those don't match up well, so they'll be extra careful, whereas with datetime-local, it's easier to make a mistake.\r\n\r\nThe specific algorithms you suggest seem like they'd make the problem worse, e.g. the first one loses the time information in the `datetime-local`, and the second one loses the timezone information in the `Date`.\r\n\r\nIn general, I think we should not expand these bad APIs further, and instead hope that the [temporal](https://github.com/tc39/proposal-temporal/) proposal makes progress. That would give a much better mapping, as discussed in https://github.com/tc39/proposal-temporal/issues/107.",
